/* =====================================================================
   teachbot.tin — TinTin++ auto-teacher for an immortal (Nilgiri)

   VERIFIED with SinGrokBot, office hours, 2026-10-31 (session 102).
   The game's flow (HELP TEACH + staff verdicts):
     1. The student follows the master; the master types
            apprentice <student>
        (Following is checked ONLY here — the MUD refuses with
        "<student> needs to be following you" if they are not.)
     2. The master types
            teach <student> "<skill>"
        QUOTES ARE REQUIRED for multi-word skills — unquoted earns
        "Your skill should be surrounded by double-quotes."
        Teach needs only the apprentice in the same room; it does
        not re-check following. Only a craft master can teach, and
        only their own apprentice.
     3. The student types  learn <points> <skill>  while the window
        is open. The window lasts ONE MUD HOUR (~75 real seconds,
        a LearnEvent); each teach starts its own timer. When it
        lapses, the skill is removed from the master — silently.
     Endings: "apprentice none" ends an apprenticeship. No
     unfollow/distance hook exists; logout probably ends it too
     (staff: not tested).

   This script automates the master's side:
     - When a player starts following you, it runs
           apprentice <player>
       and remembers them as your current apprentice.
     - When your apprentice says (or tells you)
           teach me <skill name>
       it answers with
           teach <player> "<skill name>"
       One teach per request — ask again for another window.
     - It also echoes the master's learn-feedback lines locally
       (section 3) so you can see each outcome; a lapsed window
       prints nothing in game, and the script cannot show one either.

   Load it in the immortal's tt++ session:
        #read teachbot.tin
   Switch it off/on without unloading:
        #class teachbot kill      (off)
        #class teachbot open      (on)
   See who the script currently treats as your apprentice:
        #showme $APPRENTICE

   Nilgiri display formats matched below (all log-verified):
     - Follow, two attested wordings: "X now follows you." and
       "X starts following you."
     - Speech: X says, "text"   /   X tells you, "text"
       (comma + DOUBLE quotes — single-quote patterns never fire here)
   ===================================================================== */

#class {teachbot} {open}

#variable {APPRENTICE} {}

/* ---------- 1. Follow detection -> apprentice <player> ---------- */

#action {%1 now follows you.} {
    #variable {APPRENTICE} {%1};
    apprentice %1;
    #showme {<063>TeachBot: %1 is following - apprenticed.<099>}
} {1}

#action {%1 starts following you.} {
    #variable {APPRENTICE} {%1};
    apprentice %1;
    #showme {<063>TeachBot: %1 is following - apprenticed.<099>}
} {1}

#action {%1 starts to follow you.} {
    #variable {APPRENTICE} {%1};
    apprentice %1;
    #showme {<063>TeachBot: %1 is following - apprenticed.<099>}
} {1}

/* ---------- 2. "teach me <skill>" -> teach <player> "<skill>" --- */
/* Gated to the current apprentice only (the game itself refuses to
   teach anyone else's apprentice). Stray quotes in the spoken skill
   name are stripped first, then the name is sent properly quoted;
   a trailing spoken period is dropped. */

#action {%1 says, "teach me %2"} {
    #if {"%1" == "$APPRENTICE"} {
        #replace {%2} {"} {};
        #replace {%2} {.} {};
        teach %1 "%2";
        #showme {<063>TeachBot: taught %1 "%2" - window open (~75s).<099>}
    }
} {1}

#action {%1 says, "Teach me %2"} {
    #if {"%1" == "$APPRENTICE"} {
        #replace {%2} {"} {};
        #replace {%2} {.} {};
        teach %1 "%2";
        #showme {<063>TeachBot: taught %1 "%2" - window open (~75s).<099>}
    }
} {1}

#action {%1 tells you, "teach me %2"} {
    #if {"%1" == "$APPRENTICE"} {
        #replace {%2} {"} {};
        #replace {%2} {.} {};
        teach %1 "%2";
        #showme {<063>TeachBot: taught %1 "%2" - window open (~75s).<099>}
    }
} {1}

#action {%1 tells you, "Teach me %2"} {
    #if {"%1" == "$APPRENTICE"} {
        #replace {%2} {"} {};
        #replace {%2} {.} {};
        teach %1 "%2";
        #showme {<063>TeachBot: taught %1 "%2" - window open (~75s).<099>}
    }
} {1}

/* ---------- 3. Learn feedback (display only — nothing is sent) --- */
/* The exact lines the master sees, per SinGrokBot. Single quotes
   around the skill are the game's own punctuation here. */

#action {%1 follows your example in the art of '%2'} {
    #showme {<078>TeachBot: %1 learned %2.<099>}
} {1}

#action {%1 has learned all he can in '%2' from you} {
    #showme {<078>TeachBot: %1 is capped in %2 (with you as master).<099>}
} {1}

#action {%1 has learned all she can in '%2' from you} {
    #showme {<078>TeachBot: %1 is capped in %2 (with you as master).<099>}
} {1}

#action {%1 does not seem to have improved any} {
    #showme {<078>TeachBot: %1 did not improve on that learn.<099>}
} {1}

#class {teachbot} {close}

/* =====================================================================
   Notes
   - Single-apprentice by design (the game allows the master one
     apprentice target per teach anyway): a new follower replaces
     the old slot. To teach someone who never followed, set the slot
     by hand — but remember the game checks following at apprentice
     time, so have them follow you first, then:
         #variable {APPRENTICE} {TheirName}
   - End an apprenticeship in game with:  apprentice none
     (The script's slot clears only via a new follower or by hand.)
   - Q left open with staff (asked, unanswered in the window):
     whether  teach <skill>  with no student argument also works.
     This script always sends the full two-argument form, which is
     confirmed correct.
   ===================================================================== */
